<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ferdinand I, Holy Roman Emperor</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1503">1503</a>–<a href="/years/1564">1564</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Ferdinand of Habsburg · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-habsburg">House of Habsburg</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ferdinand I was the younger brother of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a>, who gave him the Austrian lands in 1521. After Louis II died at Mohács in 1526 he was elected king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>, and Croatian nobles chose him in 1527, founding the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg Monarchy</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-europe">Central Europe</a>. He fought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-zapolya">John Zápolya</a> for Hungary and succeeded Charles as emperor in 1556.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ferdinand-i-holy-roman-emperor.jpg" alt="Ferdinand I, Holy Roman Emperor" width="320" height="401" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Hans Bocksberger the Elder · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hans_Bocksberger_der_Aeltere_001(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Ferdinand was born on 10 March 1503 at Alcalá de Henares in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a>, the second son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-the-handsome">Philip I of Castile</a> and Joanna of Castile. After his father's death in 1506 his maternal grandfather <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferdinand-ii-of-aragon">Ferdinand II of Aragon</a>, with whom he shared a name and a birthday, became his guardian, and he was raised in the royal household with an education in literature, the sciences and languages. He grew up as a patron of the arts and of scholars. He did not learn German until he was a young adult.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-ii-of-hungary">Louis II of Hungary</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a> was killed at Mohács on 29 August 1526, Ferdinand at once declared himself a candidate. On 24 October 1526 the Bohemian Diet elected him king of Bohemia on condition that he confirm the estates' privileges, but it refused to recognise him as hereditary ruler. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> a Diet of the lesser nobility proclaimed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-zapolya">John Zápolya</a>, Voivode of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transylvania">Transylvania</a>, king on 10 November 1526, and Ferdinand, supported by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a>, held the western part, which became known as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-hungary">Royal Hungary</a>.</p><p>In 1547 Ferdinand defeated a Protestant revolt in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a>, after which he centralised its administration and revoked many urban privileges. At the Diet of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/augsburg">Augsburg</a> of 1555, with authority from Charles to act and settle, he brought the delegates to agree on the principle of cuius regio, eius religio. He rushed one article through the debate and added the Declaratio Ferdinandei at the last minute, and these became flaws in the settlement. Charles abdicated in August 1556, and Ferdinand succeeded him as emperor. He founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aulic-war-council">Hofkriegsrat</a> in 1556 to coordinate military affairs in all <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> lands, but the governments of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>, Bohemia and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> stayed distinct.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In December 1562 Ferdinand had his eldest son Maximilian elected King of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a>. Ferdinand died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a> in 1564 and was buried in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prague">Prague</a>. Historians describe him as a mediocre military commander but an energetic and imaginative administrator, whose framework for his lands endured into the eighteenth century. Some historians hold that he was nearly the closest the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a> came to a Protestant emperor, while others hold that he was as Catholic as his brother but saw religion as outside politics.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ferdinand I, Holy Roman Emperor” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Archduke Ferdinand of Austria</b>, younger brother of the Emperor Charles V, had received the Austrian hereditary lands in the partition agreements of Worms (1521) and Brussels (1522), and in 1521 he married Anna of Bohemia and Hungary, while his sister Mary married her brother, King Louis II.</blockquote><a href="/empire/habsburg-monarchy">Habsburg Monarchy · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>A diet at Székesfehérvár crowned John Zápolya, voivode of Transylvania, in November 1526, while a rival diet chose <b>Ferdinand of Habsburg</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/eastern-hungarian-kingdom">Eastern Hungarian Kingdom</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1564 <b>Ferdinand I</b>'s will recreated Inner Austria for his son Charles II.</blockquote><a href="/state/inner-austria">Inner Austria</a></li><li><blockquote>On 1 January 1527 the Croatian nobles assembled at Cetin elected <b>Ferdinand of Habsburg</b> as king, in return for his help against the Ottomans.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-croatia-habsburg">Kingdom of Croatia (Habsburg)</a></li><li><blockquote>After the death of Louis II at Mohács in 1526, a diet at Pozsony elected <b>Ferdinand of Habsburg</b> king, against the rival John Zápolya.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-hungary-1526-1867">Kingdom of Hungary (1526-1867)</a></li><li><blockquote>In the contest for the Hungarian throne Jovan Nenad first backed John Zápolya, then switched to <b>Ferdinand of Habsburg</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/realm-of-jovan-nenad">Realm of Jovan Nenad</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Alcalá de Henares, in what is now <a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/austria">Austria</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=40.48,-3.36&amp;year=1542" aria-label="Alcalá de Henares on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Ferdinand I, Holy Roman Emperor"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4781 4105h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4781 4105h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=40.48,-3.36&amp;year=1542">What was here in 1542? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
